WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:08.315
Harriet Tubman is known as the mother of the Underground Railroad, the elaborate system used by African-American

2
00:00:08.316 --> 00:00:14.908
slaves to escape to freedom. She was an abolitionist, a Civil War spy, a nurse, a suffragist,

3
00:00:14.909 --> 00:00:23.965
and a humanitarian. She is one of the most influential women in American history and an icon

4
00:00:23.966 --> 00:00:28.680
who continues to inspire people fighting for equality and social justice. Tubman was named Araminta Ross when

5
00:00:28.681 --> 00:00:39.230
she was born a slave in Maryland. She was born sometime between 1820 and 1822, but the

6
00:00:39.231 --> 00:00:47.396
birthdates of most slaves were never written down, so no one is sure of her exact birthday.

7
00:00:47.397 --> 00:00:51.658
Life for slaves in the South was difficult; slave families were constantly broken up, and owners often

8
00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:59.212
abused their slaves. When Tubman was very young, her family was split up by their owner. Tubman’s

9
00:00:59.213 --> 00:01:05.593
father was forced to remain on the plantation where they'd been living while Tubman’s mother and the

10
00:01:05.594 --> 00:01:13.234
children were sent to another farm. Even as a child, Tubman suffered from the abuse of her

11
00:01:13.235 --> 00:01:19.885
masters; she was often beaten and whipped. She also sustained a head injury when an angry overseer

12
00:01:19.886 --> 00:01:27.194
threw a metal weight at a fleeing slave but hit her instead. As a result of the

13
00:01:27.195 --> 00:01:34.138
accident, Tubman suffered from headaches, seizures, and sleeping spells her whole life. Still, she worked as a

14
00:01:34.139 --> 00:01:39.776
field slave, developing a muscular build and the mental resilience that would make her famous. Like most

15
00:01:39.777 --> 00:01:45.181
slaves, Tubman was illiterate, but she developed a strong faith by listening to Bible readings. She felt

16
00:01:45.182 --> 00:01:51.996
a deep connection to the words she heard and dreamed that God spoke to her. When Tubman

17
00:01:51.997 --> 00:01:58.481
was about 22 years old, she married a free black man named John Tubman. She also changed

18
00:01:58.482 --> 00:02:05.052
her first name to Harriet to honor her mother, becoming Harriet Tubman, as she is now known.

19
00:02:05.053 --> 00:02:09.710
In 1849, she heard rumors that she would be sold and forever separated from her family. To

20
00:02:09.711 --> 00:02:18.910
avoid this fate, Tubman decided to escape, even though her husband did not want to accompany her.

21
00:02:18.911 --> 00:02:28.697
She fled to Philadelphia, traveling the early Underground Railroad routes. Recalling her arrival in Pennsylvania, she said:

22
00:02:28.698 --> 00:02:31.569
"When I found I had crossed that line, I looked at my hands to see if I

23
00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:37.457
was the same person. There was such a glory over everything; the sun came like gold through

24
00:02:37.458 --> 00:02:44.573
the trees, and over the fields, and I felt like I was in Heaven." How exactly did

25
00:02:44.574 --> 00:02:51.835
the Underground Railroad work? Its name is symbolic; this "railroad" was not really underground or a railroad.

26
00:02:51.836 --> 00:02:59.437
It was an intricate system of escape involving a long journey on foot—often three weeks or more.

27
00:02:59.438 --> 00:03:03.350
Slaves followed the North Star from the "slave states" in the South to the "free states" in

28
00:03:03.351 --> 00:03:08.334
the North or to Canada. Between 1820 and 1865, while abolitionists fought to persuade the U.S. government

29
00:03:08.335 --> 00:03:11.619
to outlaw slavery, the Railroad became the most popular way to flee the South. The dangerous journey

30
00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:16.659
took place at night, which made travelers harder to spot. People who helped the slaves were called

31
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:24.524
"conductors." They included free blacks, white abolitionists, and Christian activists. Conductors hid runaway slaves in safe houses,

32
00:03:24.525 --> 00:03:35.707
gave them food and clothing, or led them to the next safe place. The safe houses, known

33
00:03:35.708 --> 00:03:42.849
as "stations," were about 10 to 15 miles apart, although they were located closer together as the

34
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:49.674
escapees got farther north and became more tired. Routes between stations were known as "lines" and the

35
00:03:49.675 --> 00:03:54.363
fugitive slaves were referred to as "freight" or "packages." Not long after achieving freedom, Tubman heard that

36
00:03:54.364 --> 00:04:01.131
her niece and niece’s family were about to be sold. Courageously, she returned to Maryland and led

37
00:04:01.132 --> 00:04:06.884
them to Philadelphia. Over the next few years, she went back several more times, each time returning

38
00:04:06.885 --> 00:04:15.836
with more family members. Tubman wanted her husband to leave with her too, but he had remarried

39
00:04:15.837 --> 00:04:26.045
and refused to go. Inspired by her successes with her family, Tubman wanted to help others as

40
00:04:26.046 --> 00:04:36.317
a conductor in the Underground Railroad, but her task had gotten harder. In 1850, the American government

41
00:04:36.318 --> 00:04:43.430
passed the Fugitive Slave Act, which required that fugitive slaves be returned to their masters when caught.

42
00:04:43.431 --> 00:04:48.907
Runaway slaves were in greater danger than ever before, and many chose to go farther north—to Canada,

43
00:04:48.908 --> 00:04:56.041
where slavery was illegal and they were safe from slave catchers. Over the years, Tubman helped over

44
00:04:56.042 --> 00:04:58.961
three hundred slaves escape to Canada in about 19 trips. Tubman’s escape methods were recognized as brilliant,

45
00:04:58.962 --> 00:05:03.422
for she never lost any "freight." To conceal her identity, Tubman wore a maid’s clothes and, since

46
00:05:03.423 --> 00:05:07.064
she was known to be illiterate, pretended to read. For protection, she carried a loaded gun. She

47
00:05:07.065 --> 00:05:14.616
also used the gun to force frightened or tired slaves to continue north. She told them, "You’ll

48
00:05:14.617 --> 00:05:21.378
be free or die." This was for everyone’s safety, as a slave who returned home might be

49
00:05:21.379 --> 00:05:26.331
coerced into giving information about the Railroad’s routes. In October 1857, Tubman helped over forty slaves run

50
00:05:26.332 --> 00:05:34.280
away from her hometown. One owner awoke one morning to find the 15 slaves who ran his

51
00:05:34.281 --> 00:05:43.084
farm gone. Thousands of dollars were offered as reward for Tubman’s capture, but neither Tubman nor any

52
00:05:43.085 --> 00:05:49.655
of her fugitives were ever caught. People nicknamed her "Moses" because, like the prophet Moses in the

53
00:05:49.656 --> 00:05:56.588
biblical book of Exodus, she led many people to freedom. Tubman’s work with the Underground Railroad was

54
00:05:56.589 --> 00:06:02.299
interrupted by the American Civil War, which began in 1861 when 11 southern states, known as "the

55
00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:09.467
Confederacy," attempted to secede from the northern states, or "the Union." Tubman went to work for a

56
00:06:09.468 --> 00:06:14.181
Union general as a scout, spy, nurse, and cook. By helping the Union side, which wanted to

57
00:06:14.182 --> 00:06:19.458
end slavery, Tubman was still fighting for the abolitionist cause. She even led one armed assault, freeing

58
00:06:19.459 --> 00:06:27.515
over seven hundred slaves. In 1865, the Union triumphed, and slavery ended with the war. After the

59
00:06:27.516 --> 00:06:36.096
war, Tubman moved to New York State, remarried, and devoted herself to speaking out for the women’s

60
00:06:36.097 --> 00:06:45.597
suffrage movement. Sometime in the 1890s, she underwent brain surgery, choosing to bite down on a bullet

61
00:06:45.598 --> 00:06:53.035
instead of receiving anesthesia. A Boston newspaper honored her for her achievements, but her life’s work had

62
00:06:53.036 --> 00:06:57.485
left her so impoverished that she had to sell a cow just to buy a train ticket

63
00:06:57.486 --> 00:07:03.142
to the ceremony. Despite her financial struggles, she donated land and later had a rest home built

64
00:07:03.143 --> 00:07:09.220
on it for aging and needy black people. In 1911, she was admitted to this very same

65
00:07:09.221 --> 00:07:13.958
rest home. She died penniless on March 10, 1913, surrounded by loved ones. Tubman’s last words were

66
00:07:13.959 --> 00:07:17.452
from the Bible: "I go to prepare a place for you." Until her final breath, she was

67
00:07:17.453 --> 00:07:23.555
thinking about God and other people. She was buried with full military honors in Auburn, New York.

68
00:07:23.556 --> 00:07:29.115
Dozens of schools, museums, and centers throughout North America have since been opened in her name, and

69
00:07:29.116 --> 00:07:31.671
prestigious military academies study her legendary escape strategies to this day. Harriet Tubman was a true hero.

